WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.039
The end of Frasier back in 2004 hasn't exactly
stopped Jane Leeves who played Daphne on the

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.050
show from working consistently in Hollywood.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.400
From her animated film and voice work to the
next generation of television comedies, here's

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.980
what she's been up to over the years.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:19.240
Leeves is arguably best known for her role
on Frasier as Daphne Moon, a physical therapist

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.039
who truly believes she has psychic powers.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.200
“Oh wait a minute…

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.100
I’m getting something on you.”

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:26.800
“She’s psychic.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.680
We’ve decided to find it charming.”

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.000
Leeves became a household name during her
time on the series, picking up Emmy and Golden

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.940
Globe nominations throughout the show's run.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:39.220
Offscreen, Frasier turned Leeves into the
highest-paid British actress at the time;

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.900
while onscreen, viewers became extraordinarily
invested in Daphne's romantic relationship

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.640
with Niles Crane, Frasier's brother.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.070
This relationship is developed while she lives
in the Cranes' apartment as a caretaker to

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.580
their father Martin, despite the fact that
Niles is married when they first meet.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.830
Though some critics weren't as enamored with
the relationship, it helped sell Leeves as

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:03.240
a romantic lead and when the show ended in
2004, the finale was well-received, leaving

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.890
Leeves and the rest of the main cast in a
prime position to pursue whichever projects

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.220
they wanted to tackle next.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.520
Having become a TV star, Leeves opted for
a relatively modest slate of film and television

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.060
work following her Frasier breakthrough.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.560
Using her distinctive voice and now-famous
British accent, Leeves lent her voice to a

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.600
number of animated films, beginning with the
stop-motion film James &amp; the Giant Peach in

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:24.560
1996.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.520
Based on Roald Dahl's famous children's novel,
Peach featured Leeves as Mrs. Ladybug, one

00:01:29.531 --> 00:01:34.240
of James' insect friends, alongside big names
like Susan Sarandon, Richard Dreyfuss, and

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.200
David Thewlis.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.380
Her next animated role was a return to television
in the Penguins of Madagascar series, where

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.400
she played Lulu the chimp for two episodes
in both 2009 and 2011.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.840
She followed this up with recurring and varied
voice work on Phineas &amp; Ferb, where she appeared

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.720
as Wanda Acronym, one of the leaders of the
Organization Without a Cool Acronym; she's

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.520
in charge of a secret agent that also happens
to be someone's pet chihuahua.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:57.920
It’s kind of a weird show.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:03.620
During Frasier's run, Leeves booked a role
in 1999's Music of the Heart, a feature film

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.310
based on the true story of Roberta Guaspari
a crusader for music education within New

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.510
York City's public schools, who pioneered
music as a source of inspiration and stability

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.380
for at-risk youth.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:17.620
Directed by Wes Craven, the film was nominated
for a small handful of Academy Awards and

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.090
was well-received by critics.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.540
In 2002, Leeves made her Broadway debut, taking
over for Molly Ringwald in the long-running

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:24.540
show Cabaret.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:29.280
She also appeared in the 2003 film The Event,
a low-budget indie that premiered at Sundance

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.480
to standing ovations.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.960
Thanks to their success on Frasier, Leeves
and her fellow female lead Peri Gilpin decided

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:39.220
to take charge of their own Hollywood fates
and start their own production company.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.590
The two subsequently founded Bristol Cities,
which Leeves later confessed was named after

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:44.940
a dirty Cockney rhyme.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.100
The company's most notable project was intended
to be the American remake of the popular and

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:53.650
beloved British series The Vicar of Dibley,
which the two produced in 2007.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:58.510
The original, which ran for four years in
the 1990s on BBC One, focused on a small fictional

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.300
English village called Dibley and its female
vicar.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.060
With star Kirstie Alley and producer Richard
Curtis on board, it seemed a surefire success

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.240
but Fox sadly ended up passing on the pilot.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.480
The 2000s saw Leeves maintain a steady presence
on television, beginning with a two-episode

00:03:13.489 --> 00:03:17.880
arc on Desperate Housewives as a therapist
who helps Lynette and Tom with their relationship

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:18.380
problems.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.680
Much more recently, she's also appeared on
Fox's medical drama The Resident, becoming

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.819
a series regular for the second season as
orthopedic surgeon Dr. Kitt Voss.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.300
However, her biggest post-Frasier gig has
undoubtedly been her leading role on Hot in

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.880
Cleveland as Joy Scroggs, a forty-something
former "eyebrow artist to the stars."

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:41.720
The series, which aired on TV Land for 128
episodes between 2010 and 2015, also starred

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.519
Leeves’ Frasier co-star Wendie Malick, and
told the story of four best friends from Los

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.680
Angeles who end up making a new life in Cleveland.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.260
Former Frasier stars Peri Gilpin and John
Mahoney also guest starred on the show, which

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.380
was well-received, earning SAG and Primetime
Emmy nominations, and wins, throughout its

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:56.880
run.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:02.260
In 1996, Leeves married a television executive
named Marshall Coben, and when she became

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:06.500
pregnant with their first child, Isabella,
during her time on Frasier, the writers adapted

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.310
quickly, writing her pregnancy into a plot
that found the character gaining weight thanks

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.370
to her stressful relationship with Niles.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.220
The couple's second child, Finn, was born
in 2003.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.260
Leeves' pregnancy being a part of Frasier
isn't the only impact that the show had on

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:20.269
her family life.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:24.370
Peri Gilpin and Leeves have been close friends
for a number of years, and not only was Gilpin

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:28.490
present at the birth of Leeves' daughter,
but she's also Isabella's godmother, and Leeves

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.580
is godmother to Gilpin's daughter, Stella.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.360
The two former co-workers even live next door
to each other.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:38.180
When Leeves' son Finn was born, she named
John Mahoney and David Hyde Pierce as his

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:38.680
godfathers.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.940
“Turn him over if he starts fussing, will
you dad?”

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:41.460
“No.”

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:45.940
“I’m role playing dad.”

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.520
“Try playing the role of a sane person.”

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:53.000
Clearly, the relationships on Frasier went
far beyond the set itself, and began relationships

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.660
between the cast members that would go on
to become the foundations for a real-life

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:57.160
family.

